  "semantic_checklist": [
    {
      "id": "C01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The attached printed pages 1, 3, 5, and 6 establish the constant-coefficient system, hyperbolicity, Riemann data, and spectral solvability sentence crossing pages 5–6.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation concerns a constant real matrix with a real eigenbasis and a spectral traveling-step construction for Riemann data.",
      "reasoning": "The selected passage and inherited context match the subject of the translation. Source bytes and rendering hashes were supplied by the orchestrator, not independently recomputed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 1 and 3 use a real m-dimensional state space, a real square matrix, and a complete set of real eigenvectors.",
      "translation_evidence": "The binders use a finite index type ι, states in ℝ^ι, a square real matrix, real eigenvalues, and an indexed real basis.",
      "reasoning": "Finite coordinate indexing represents the source's finite-dimensional real state space. The additional origin state specifies otherwise unspecified point values."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source allows constant left and right states and asserts solvability using spectral data of the given hyperbolic matrix.",
      "translation_evidence": "For every matrix satisfying the eigenbasis hypothesis and every L,O,R, spectral witnesses exist and all conclusions concern their single constructed function.",
      "reasoning": "The quantifiers cover arbitrary source data. Allowing the spectral witnesses to depend on the states does not remove a source requirement: the passage does not demand a simultaneously selected basis before all initial data."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 3 requires real eigenvalues and m independent corresponding eigenvectors, without distinctness, sorting, or normalization requirements.",
      "translation_evidence": "D001 requires a complete real eigenbasis and imposes no distinctness, ordering, orthogonality, or sign restriction.",
      "reasoning": "The governing algebraic hypothesis matches hyperbolicity. No classical differentiability across jumps is imposed. Positive time is a local condition on similarity only."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 5–6 assert that spectral data solve the Riemann problem; printed page 5 identifies integral conservation as the formulation surviving discontinuities.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation supplies spectral steps, initial data, similarity, and an integral balance with respect to μ, but D080 leaves the underlying measure unresolved.",
      "reasoning": "The geometric and initial-data conclusions are present. Whether the analytic conclusion represents the source's conservation law cannot be settled without identifying μ."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source uses ordinary real matrix action, eigenvector decomposition, and spatial and temporal conservation integrals.",
      "translation_evidence": "D001–D094 explain matrix action, basis coordinates, step profiles, finite sums, and oriented integrals. D080 explicitly reports that the measure instance and its normalization are not expanded.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied ledger makes the algebraic operators intelligible, but a central analytic dependency remains unresolved. Its intended meaning cannot be inferred solely from its name."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source asserts spectral solvability and describes similarity and finitely many constant-speed waves; its general hyperbolic similarity statement is qualified by 'typically'.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation asserts exact integral and pointwise equalities for a particular formula, including all-real-time conservation and positive-time similarity.",
      "reasoning": "The unconditional similarity claim is restricted to the linear setting, so it does not incorrectly remove the qualification for general nonlinear systems. The strength of the analytic refinement remains unresolved because its measure is unspecified."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 3 identifies eigenvectors as decomposition directions and eigenvalues as propagation speeds; equation (1.11) supplies the two initial states.",
      "translation_evidence": "The same A appears in the eigenvector equations and flux. Each basis-coordinate profile of L,O,R multiplies its corresponding eigenvector and travels at its corresponding eigenvalue.",
      "reasoning": "The matrix, spectral data, coordinates, state vectors, flux, and constructed solution retain distinct and consistent roles."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source places the interface at x=0 and initial time at t=0, identifies eigenvalues as wave speeds, and describes dependence on x/t.",
      "translation_evidence": "The formula uses x−λ_p t, sums over every basis index, and states q(x,t)=q(x/t,1) for t>0.",
      "reasoning": "The propagation sign agrees with q_t+Aq_x=0. Reference time 1 expresses similarity without introducing a physical coefficient. No unsupported eigenvalue ordering is imposed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected claim concerns finite-dimensional real linear hyperbolic systems and initial evolution from t=0.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation retains real finite-dimensional linear systems and additionally allows an empty index type, equal states, arbitrary origin values, and conservation at negative times.",
      "reasoning": "These are identified extensions, not applicability restrictions. Positive-time similarity covers the forward initial-value interpretation. None extends the spectral construction to nonlinear systems."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.11) leaves x=0 unspecified. The source allows repeated eigenvalues and does not require classical PDE validity at jumps.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation prescribes arbitrary O initially and its basis coordinates on characteristic lines; repeated and zero eigenvalues are allowed, and division occurs only for t>0.",
      "reasoning": "The pointwise convention is an explicit construction choice rather than a source-prescribed value. With ordinary Lebesgue integration, moving-front point values do not alter temporal flux integrals, and stationary-mode contributions have zero flux. The unresolved μ prevents verifying these boundary semantics for the actual conservation predicate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source includes nontrivial hyperbolic matrices and discontinuous initial states.",
      "translation_evidence": "The hypotheses admit nonempty finite index sets, diagonal real matrices, and distinct L,R. Integrability is explicitly required, but D080 does not identify μ.",
      "reasoning": "The algebraic premises are satisfiable and the construction is not confined to the empty-dimensional case. However, an unidentified measure could make the conservation assertion analytically uninformative; for example, zero measure would make every displayed integral vanish."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 5–6 assert solution through eigenvalues and eigenvectors, with finitely many constant-speed waves.",
      "translation_evidence": "D003–D006 construct q as a finite sum of basis-coordinate step profiles translated at their eigenvalues.",
      "reasoning": "The translated statement remains directly linked to the spectral solution construction. It does not substitute an unrelated numerical discretization."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source uses ordinary finite-dimensional real vectors and their eigenvector decomposition, without a norm estimate or orthogonality requirement.",
      "translation_evidence": "D038–D039 supply a finite-product normed structure for integration; D068 identifies coefficients as basis coordinates.",
      "reasoning": "The norm supplies analytic structure rather than changing an asserted estimate. Basis coordinates are correctly distinguished from original vector components."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected linear spectral-solvability claim contains no error bound, conditioning claim, or stability estimate.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation contains exact solution identities and no error or stability quantity.",
      "reasoning": "There is no error or stability notion to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected sentence concerns solving the linear Riemann problem; the subsequent page separately discusses approximate solvers for nonlinear problems.",
      "translation_evidence": "All spectral data, profiles, and equalities are exact real mathematical objects.",
      "reasoning": "The translation preserves the exact linear solution role and does not import the neighboring approximate-solver discussion."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage asserts no asymptotic expansion, remainder, or convergence rate.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation contains no asymptotic or higher-order terms.",
      "reasoning": "No asymptotic status needs preservation. The initial condition is specified directly at t=0 in both accounts."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source asserts spectral solvability, explicitly requires a complete eigenbasis, and does not state PDE uniqueness in a specified function space.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation gives existential spectral witnesses and a constructed integrable solution candidate, without asserting PDE uniqueness. Its solution predicate uses the unresolved μ.",
      "reasoning": "Spectral completeness and the absence of an added uniqueness claim match. Verification that the constructed witness is a solution in the source's integral-conservation sense remains dependent on identifying the measure."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The eigenbasis, traveling-step formula, initial states, positive-time similarity, and finite constant-speed wave structure align with the source. With ordinary Lebesgue integration, the stated balance would supply the required integral solution semantics. The supplied translation explicitly leaves the real measure unidentified, so the analytic conclusion cannot presently be certified as the source's conservation law. Independently repairing that evidence gap by assuming a familiar instance is prohibited."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The source supports real spectral decomposition and solvability but does not display this formula or specify the origin convention, all-real-time rectangular identity, or sectionwise integrability. These are plausible constructive refinements rather than demonstrated contradictions. Their derivation requires a definite interpretation of the measure and a check that the chosen point values preserve every rectangular balance, including stationary characteristics. The supplied evidence leaves that interpretation unresolved."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-measure-semantics",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 5 requires the integral conservation law to continue holding at discontinuities; the selected Riemann claim therefore includes substantive conservation semantics.",
      "translation_evidence": "D080 is explicitly marked unclear, and the translation defines every conservation integral using μ=Real.measureSpace.volume without establishing that measure's construction or normalization.",
      "impact": "The integral predicate cannot be identified conclusively with the source's conservation law. This affects implication, boundary-value, and analytic nonvacuity checks and requires adjudication using authoritative dependency evidence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "explicit-construction-refinements",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 5–6 defer the spectral construction to Chapter 3; equation (1.11) leaves the interface value unspecified.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation supplies a particular finite spectral formula, arbitrary origin data, characteristic-line values, and conservation over all real temporal endpoints.",
      "impact": "These additions do not restrict the original left and right states and are not independently evidence of unfaithfulness. They must be assessed as construction choices and extensions, rather than attributed as explicit statements on the selected pages."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Independent inspection of the attached source pages supports the translation's algebraic setting, hyperbolicity assumption, initial half-line data, and spectral wave structure. No definite substantive mismatch is established. Acceptance is nevertheless premature because the blind translation itself leaves the measure underlying its central solution predicate unresolved. Adjudication should identify that dependency and then determine whether the explicit construction and boundary conventions yield the source's integral solution, and whether the additional assertions are equivalent refinements or genuine strengthening."
